{"meta": {"limit": 500, "next": null, "offset": 0, "previous": null, "total_count": 29}, "objects": [{"abstract": "   This document describes use cases and applicability for Discovery of\n   Agents With Names (DAWN).  It illustrates how clients discover AI\n   resources and obtain the minimum information needed for subsequent\n   interaction within a local network, within an organisation, or\n   between cooperating organisations with trust relationships.\n\n   This document does not define a discovery protocol, a registration\n   procedure, a selection algorithm, or an agent-to-agent communication\n   protocol.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-03-14T10:00:18Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 157082, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-kay-dawn-use-cases", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 26, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-kay-dawn-use-cases/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/168849/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/164065/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-09-10T10:00:18Z", "title": "Use Cases and Applicability for Discovery of Agents With Names", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   The proliferation of distributed systems, Artificial Intelligence\n   (AI) agents, and network services has created a need for\n   interoperable mechanisms to discover entities across administrative\n   and network boundaries.  Entities may include AI agents, software\n   services, and other named resources that need to be found and\n   characterised before interaction can begin.\n\n   This document defines the requirements for Discovery of Agents With\n   Names (DAWN) and sets out the objectives that a discovery mechanism\n   for such entities must satisfy.  It describes what information must\n   be discoverable, what properties a discovery mechanism needs to\n   support, and what constraints apply to discovery in decentralised\n   environments.\n\n   This document does not specify any particular discovery protocol or\n   solution.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-22T07:52:43Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 156149, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-king-dawn-requirements", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 10, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-king-dawn-requirements/", "rev": "02", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/162091/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/162685/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/170566/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-19T07:52:43Z", "title": "Requirements for the Discovery of Agents With Names (DAWN)", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Interacting entities such as agents, tasks, users, workloads, data,\n   compute, etc., in AI ecosystem/network are proliferating, yet there\n   is no standardised way to discover what entities exist, what\n   attributes such as skills, capabilities, physical characteristics,\n   etc., they posses, what services they offer, or how to reach them\n   across organisational boundaries.\n\n   Discovery today relies on proprietary directories or manual\n   configuration, creating fragmented ecosystems that prevent cross-\n   domain collaboration.\n\n   This document describes the problem space that motivates Discovery of\n   Agents, Workloads, and Named Entities (DAWN).  It clarifies the scope\n   of work within entity ecosystems, identifies why current approaches\n   are insufficient, and outlines the challenges a standardised\n   discovery mechanism must address.  It does not propose a specific\n   solution or protocol.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-01-20T11:03:54Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 156148, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-akhavain-moussa-dawn-problem-statement", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 22, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-akhavain-moussa-dawn-problem-statement/", "rev": "05", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/162090/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/163205/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/163356/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/163979/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/164371/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/166438/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-07-19T11:03:54Z", "title": "Problem Statement for the Discovery of Agents, Workloads, and Named Entities (DAWN)", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document describes the privacy issues associated with the\n   Discovery of Agents, Workloads, and Named Entities (DAWN).  It\n   provides general observations about typical current privacy practices\n   in similar domains like, DNS, HTTP, and in general privacy in\n   information retrieval.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-11-23T08:00:43Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 156783, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-iannone-dawn-privacy-considerations", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 8, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-iannone-dawn-privacy-considerations/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/163409/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-05-22T08:00:43Z", "title": "Privacy Considerations for the Discovery of Agents, Workloads, and Named Entities (DAWN)", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This Internet-Draft provides a gap analysis and applicability\n   statement that maps existing Discovery Mechanisms and protocols to\n   the DAWN problem statement and discovery requirements.  The analysis\n   evaluates security, privacy, applicability, flexibility, and\n   suitability of existing standardized, non-standardized, and proposed\n   discovery solutions and protocols to the DAWN problem statement and\n   REQ-DISC corpus.  It identifies high priority gaps, proposes\n   mitigations and hybrid patterns, and serves as reference for future\n   prototyping and development.  The intention is for this draft to\n   evolve as new solutions come to existence.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-12-14T19:44:30Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 157170, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-moussa-dawn-gap-analysis", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 37, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-moussa-dawn-gap-analysis/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/164370/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/164281/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-06-12T19:44:30Z", "title": "Gap Analysis and Applicability Statement for Discovery Protocols of Agents, Workloads, and Named Entities (DAWN)", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   The Internet of Agents (IoA) raises a question beyond IPv6 address\n   space and end-to-end connectivity: should an IPv6 network be able to\n   relate packet forwarding to agent capability, policy, and short-lived\n   execution state?  This informational document states the problem,\n   sketches a framework for agent-aware IPv6 forwarding, and lists open\n   questions for community discussion.  It does not define packet\n   formats, routing extensions, IANA allocations, or a new agent\n   discovery protocol.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-01-04T04:08:00Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 157677, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-he-dawn-ipv6-agent-aware-framework", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 12, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-he-dawn-ipv6-agent-aware-framework/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/165437/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-07-03T04:08:00Z", "title": "Agent-Awareness in IPv6 Networks: Problem Statement and Framework", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document surveys mechanisms for AI agent discovery being\n   developed at the IETF, at AAIF, at 3GPP, and in open-source projects.\n   It compares them by discovery model, scope, dynamism, cross-domain\n   reach, and semantic capability.  It identifies gaps, overlaps, and\n   conflicts between the approaches to inform future standardization\n   work.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-01-04T12:39:07Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 157695, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-jimenez-dawn-discovery-landscape", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 29, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-jimenez-dawn-discovery-landscape/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/165500/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-07-03T12:39:07Z", "title": "A Survey of AI Agent Discovery Mechanisms", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   The proliferation of distributed Artificial Intelligence (AI) systems\n   and agents has created a need for interoperable mechanisms for\n   components of AI systems to discover each other.\n\n   This document defines terminology for Discovery of Agents With Names\n   (DAWN), focusing on the terms necessary to describe the discovery of\n   information about AI systems and resources (e.g., AI agents).  The\n   intention is that this common set of terms can be used by other\n   documents related to DAWN and so achieve consistency of meaning\n   across the space.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-06T16:42:44Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 156217, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-farrel-dawn-terminology", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 9, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-farrel-dawn-terminology/", "rev": "07", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/166968/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/162209/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/162413/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/163951/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/165680/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/170575/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171132/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171988/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-11-02T16:42:44Z", "title": "Terminology for the Discovery of Agents With Names (DAWN)", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Protocols for communication between autonomous software agents\n   typically discover agents through documents retrieved over HTTPS from\n   a well-known URI.  This model requires a discovering party to already\n   know an agent's host name or URL, or to consult a centralized\n   catalog.  It provides no zero-configuration mechanism for enumerating\n   agents that are present on a local network.\n\n   This document describes how existing, widely deployed protocols,\n   Multicast DNS (mDNS) and DNS-Based Service Discovery (DNS-SD), can be\n   used to advertise and discover agents on a local link, with no new\n   protocol machinery.  It presents a general, protocol-independent two-\n   stage discovery model in which lightweight enumeration over DNS-SD is\n   followed by obtaining full agent metadata over the agent's native\n   transport, whether by retrieving a description document or by\n   connecting to the agent and performing its native handshake.  The\n   DNS-SD records carry a small set of discovery parameters that is\n   shared with agent discovery in unicast DNS.  The document gives\n   worked instantiations of that model for the Agent2Agent (A2A)\n   protocol and the Model Context Protocol (MCP).\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-01T07:52:21Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 157914, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-jakab-dawn-agent-discovery-mdns", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 34, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-jakab-dawn-agent-discovery-mdns/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/166218/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171113/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-28T07:52:21Z", "title": "Zero-Configuration Agent Discovery", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines a DNS-like three-tier agent-discovery\n   architecture for the Internet of Agents (IoA).  It introduces three\n   core functional roles: Agent Root, Agent Registry, and Agent\n   Resolver.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-03-21T17:38:53Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 157887, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-yao-dawn-agent-discovery-architect", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 10, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-yao-dawn-agent-discovery-architect/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/166112/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/169201/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-09-17T17:38:53Z", "title": "DNS-like Agent Discovery Architecture", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document specifies a mechanism for resource discovery across\n   cooperating administrative domains.  It defines how discovery\n   requests preserve their scope and constraints across referrals, how\n   discovery services report coverage of requested scopes, and how\n   clients combine results from multiple discovery branches.  The\n   mechanism enables clients to distinguish complete discovery results\n   from incomplete operations, including cases involving unresolved\n   referrals, pagination failures, and partial service failures.  It\n   relies on existing authentication, authorization, and trust\n   arrangements and does not define open-ended Internet search, semantic\n   matchmaking, resource invocation, registration, or directory\n   synchronization.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-03T11:12:04Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 162052, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-cui-dawn-cooperating-discovery", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 23, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-cui-dawn-cooperating-discovery/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/171331/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-30T11:12:04Z", "title": "Resource Discovery in Cooperating Domains", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Agent Identity and Discovery (AID) answers one question: given a\n   domain, where is the agent and which protocol should a client speak?\n   An AID client queries a DNS TXT record at the well-known subdomain\n   _agent.<domain> and learns the service endpoint URI, protocol token,\n   authentication hint, and optional metadata for that agent.\n\n   This document defines the AID v2 (`aid2`) record format, client\n   discovery algorithm, exact-host lookup rules, endpoint-proof (PKA)\n   handshake using Ed25519 HTTP Message Signatures, security\n   requirements, and IANA registrations for the `_agent` DNS node name\n   and the `agent` service name.  The legacy `aid1` record format is\n   retained as a compatibility format for clients migrating from earlier\n   deployments.  AID is intentionally small; after discovery, protocol-\n   specific mechanisms such as MCP or A2A handle communication and\n   capability negotiation.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-03-06T09:33:33Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 160868, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-nemethi-dawn-aid", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 29, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-nemethi-dawn-aid/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/168470/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-09-02T09:33:33Z", "title": "Agent Identity and Discovery (AID)", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document specifies the integration architecture, discovery proxy\n   mechanisms, and operational workflows for the Agent Gateway (AGW)\n   within the Discovery of Agents With Names (DAWN) problem space.\n   Addressing the core problem of how an agent discovers peer agents\n   across administrative and network boundaries, this specification\n   defines the dual role of the Agent Gateway: serving as an Outbound\n   Discovery Proxy (Discovery Substrate Access Point) that assists local\n   domain agents in querying external discovery fabrics, and an Inbound\n   Domain Discovery Proxy (Broker/Aggregator) that enables external\n   agents to discover matching capabilities situated on sub-agents\n   behind the gateway.  The document outlines the internal capability\n   matching engine, selective disclosure mechanisms for privacy\n   preservation, DNS-SD/mDNS resource record mappings, and strict\n   boundary alignments with the IETF DAWN Working Group Charter.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-06T15:38:05Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 162227, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-miao-dawn-agw-intergration-consideration", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 20, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-miao-dawn-agw-intergration-consideration/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/171944/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-11-02T15:38:05Z", "title": "Considerations for Agent Gateway Integration within the DAWN Framework", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document describes a two-layer reference architecture for\n   discovering the minimum public information needed to reach AI agents.\n   The Local Discovery Plane obtains discovery information within an\n   administrative domain through mDNS/DNS-SD or infrastructure-assisted\n   discovery.  For cross-domain discovery, the Federation Plane uses\n   publication of policy-approved information in authoritative DNS zones\n   and on-demand resolution as its baseline; Federation Gateways apply\n   export policy and validate and cache accepted results.  The document\n   also identifies optional functions that may complement this baseline\n   where DNS publication and request-driven resolution do not meet\n   deployment needs: recipient-specific sharing, proactive or multi-hop\n   dissemination, and trusted aggregated directory queries.  In mixed-\n   mode deployments, DNS may bootstrap an endpoint that provides one of\n   these additional functions.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-24T02:34:02Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 160908, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-zhang-dawn-agent-discovery-framework", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 37, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-zhang-dawn-agent-discovery-framework/", "rev": "09", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/170401/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/170404/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/170145/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/168619/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/168761/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/169927/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/170068/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/170025/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/170558/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/170659/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-21T02:34:02Z", "title": "A Framework for Agent Discovery in DAWN", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document specifies the Federated Agent Metadata Synchronization\n   Protocol (FAMS-P), a normative protocol realization of the \"BGP-like\n   structured peering\" federation mode (Mode B) defined in the two-layer\n   DAWN discovery architecture (draft-zhang-dawn-agent-discovery-\n   framework).  FAMS-P is a peer-to-peer unicast protocol for\n   synchronizing agent discovery metadata between Federation Gateways\n   (FGWs) across administrative domains, in deployments where the\n   preferred DNS delegation model does not meet operational\n   requirements, such as closed trusted federations of approximately 2\n   to 200 sites.\n\n   FAMS-P runs over mutually-authenticated TLS 1.3 sessions between\n   peering gateways.  It supports initial full metadata synchronization\n   and incremental updates for agent online, offline, and change events.\n   Each gateway maintains a local Federated Agent Directory (FAD).  The\n   protocol synchronizes only lightweight Federation Metadata Records\n   (FMRs) — a concrete binding of the DAWN Minimum Discoverable\n   Information (MDI) — and thus transfers only the minimum public\n   discovery information permitted by the DAWN charter; richer\n   capability retrieval is out of scope.\n\n   The protocol defines complete message formats, a session state\n   machine with connection collision resolution, timestamp-and-replay-\n   protected conflict resolution, mandatory FMR origin signatures, time-\n   to-live aging with bounded refresh jitter, and fine-grained per-peer\n   export policy enforcement.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-17T08:31:06Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161588, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-zhang-dawn-bgp-style-federated-discovery", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 26, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-zhang-dawn-bgp-style-federated-discovery/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/170314/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-14T08:31:06Z", "title": "Federated Agent Metadata Synchronization Protocol (FAMS-P)", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines parameters for AI agents that would be used in\n   DNS and mDNS discovery processes.  It provides a set of common\n   parameters for different DNS records to facilitate agents\n   communicating over application layer protocols such as A2A or MCP.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-30T09:08:29Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161928, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-blwm-dawn-common-discovery-parameters", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 27, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-blwm-dawn-common-discovery-parameters/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/171037/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-27T09:08:29Z", "title": "Common Agent DNS Discovery Parameters", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   DAWN defines discovery as an initial step that returns minimum\n   discoverable information (MDI) about entities.  Clients, however,\n   usually start from an intent, not from a name or a capability, and\n   the capabilities needed to satisfy an intent depend on what is\n   available.  A client therefore has to know what is on offer to\n   decide what to look for, and has to decide what to look for to learn\n   what is on offer.\n\n   This document describes that circular dependency, locates it in the\n   current DAWN documents, compares five strategies for handling it,\n   and lists questions for the Architecture work.  The main question is\n   what the MDI must contain for a client to act without a pre-agreed\n   capability vocabulary.  This document proposes no protocol.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-01T07:42:37Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161957, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-hecker-dawn-intent-capability-gap", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 9, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-hecker-dawn-intent-capability-gap/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/171112/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-28T07:42:37Z", "title": "The Intent-to-Capability Knowledge Gap in Discovery of Agents With Names", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines a JSON discovery document, served at a well-\n   known URI, that carries the public properties a client needs before\n   using a capability index: its name, the protocol versions it serves,\n   the location of its catalogue, and the key that signs the catalogue.\n   It also states the requirements for that signed catalogue and defines\n   one signature profile.  Discovery between indexes, catalogue\n   exchange, trust, and search or selection among catalogued\n   capabilities are out of scope.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-06T19:29:26Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 162041, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-artamokhov-dawn-marketplace-discovery", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 18, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-artamokhov-dawn-marketplace-discovery/", "rev": "03", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/171324/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171295/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171879/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/172079/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-11-02T19:29:26Z", "title": "A Discovery Document and Signed Catalogue for Capability Marketplaces", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document describes how independently operated capability indexes\n   discover each other by following discovery documents, how a reading\n   index identifies itself to the index it reads, and an admission model\n   that distinguishes a peer that is known from a peer whose catalogue\n   is indexed.  It also describes how an index can preview a peer's\n   catalogue before admitting it.  Payment, identity, reputation and\n   invocation are out of scope.  The document is intended as input to\n   the DAWN gap analysis on federation between directories.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-03T10:49:22Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 162042, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-artamokhov-dawn-index-federation", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 13, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-artamokhov-dawn-index-federation/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/171325/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171296/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-30T10:49:22Z", "title": "Federation Between Capability Indexes: Crawl, Reciprocal Identification and Admission", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   The agentic AI ecosystem includes many different technologies and\n   operations.  One important aspect is the ability to establish\n   connections between AI agents.  In single-platform approaches, the\n   processes and metadata with which AI agents discover, locate, and\n   connect to each other are typically managed by their common platform.\n   This document describes an inter-domain mechanism to enable AI agents\n   to locate and connect to each other using necessary metadata and\n   secure DNS associations, in a similar fashion to the way that DNS-\n   Based Authentication of Named Entities (DANE), RFC6698, does for\n   Transport Layer Security.  This is accomplished through two new\n   Resource Record (RR) types: AI Discovery Association (AIDISCA) and AI\n   Index (AIINDEX).\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-03T15:27:10Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 157062, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-seethiraju-dawn-dan", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 21, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-seethiraju-dawn-dan/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/164007/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171377/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-30T15:27:10Z", "title": "DNS-Based Agent Naming (DAN): AIDISCA and AIINDEX Resource Records for AI Agent Discovery", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   In an agent system, agents need to discover other agents, tool\n   servers, and skills.  Agent Resource Discovery (ARD) defines a\n   manifest listing such resources and a lookup interface over it.  This\n   document specifies how a client (such as an agent) initially\n   discovers an ARD manifest.  This discovery is performed through the\n   use of DNS Service Binding records, DNS-Based Service Discovery (DNS-\n   SD), HTTP link relations, or well-known URIs.  Each mechanism yields\n   a set of manifest URIs.  The manifest format, the lookup interface,\n   and the binding of a manifest to its publisher are specified in the\n   companion ARD documents.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-03T16:34:26Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 162071, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-jimenez-dawn-ard-initial-contact", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 24, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-jimenez-dawn-ard-initial-contact/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/171390/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-30T16:34:26Z", "title": "Agent Resource Discovery (ARD): Initial Contact", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document describes the high-level architecture for Agent\n   Resource Discovery (ARD), an open, federated framework for\n   publishing, indexing, discovering, and invoking software agent\n   capabilities (such as Model Context Protocol servers, Agent-to-Agent\n   agents, programmatic skills, and web APIs) across local networks,\n   enterprise domains, and the public Internet.  It defines the system\n   roles, deployment topologies, core architectural principles, and end-\n   to-end interaction flows that connect the ARD specifications for\n   establishing Initial Contact and the Discovery Interface for\n   capability lookup in the ARD catalog.  The Registration interface\n   will be specified in a later document.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-03T16:38:20Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 162072, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-bu-dawn-ard-architecture", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 15, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-bu-dawn-ard-architecture/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/171391/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-30T16:38:20Z", "title": "Agent Resource Discovery (ARD) Architecture", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document specifies the catalog representation and lookup\n   protocol for Agent Resource Discovery (ARD), an open architecture for\n   publishing, discovering, and searching software agent capabilities\n   across federated networks.  ARD defines a JSON-LD capability\n   description envelope (the ARD Entry) and catalog container (the ARD\n   Manifest) identified by domain-anchored Uniform Resource Names\n   (URNs), cryptographic publisher authority bindings (the Trust\n   Manifest), and an HTTP query interface supporting structured,\n   semantic, and federated search across registries.  Initial contact\n   for discovering catalog entry points and active agent registration\n   are specified in companion ARD documents.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-03T16:43:03Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 162074, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-bu-dawn-ard-discovery", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 32, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-bu-dawn-ard-discovery/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/171392/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-30T16:43:03Z", "title": "Agent Resource Discovery (ARD): Manifest and Lookup", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document describes an infrastructure-assisted approach\n   to the initial discovery of AI agents in managed enterprise,\n   cloud, edge-computing, and communication-network\n   environments. The approach treats infrastructure-provided\n   resource state and requester context as possible inputs to\n   the Minimum Viable Dataset (MVD) used by a discovery\n   mechanism. These inputs can help keep minimum public\n   discovery information current and can help determine the\n   appropriate discovery scope or entry point without requiring\n   the requester to understand deployment-specific network\n   details. The document does not define registration, catalog\n   search, semantic matchmaking, resource ranking, or selection,\n   and does not define a new discovery wire protocol.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-05T04:41:25Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 161579, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-dunbar-dawn-infra-assisted-discovery", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 48, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-dunbar-dawn-infra-assisted-discovery/", "rev": "03", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/170439/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/170488/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/170291/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171581/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-11-01T04:41:25Z", "title": "Infrastructure-Assisted Discovery for AI Agents", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Large Language Model (LLM) API services can be hosted on\n   workstations, local servers, and other devices in a local network.\n   AI applications commonly access these services through manually\n   configured API base URLs and interface settings.  When an application\n   joins a different network or a service address changes, these\n   settings may need to be updated even when a suitable local service is\n   already available.\n\n   This document specifies a DNS-Based Service Discovery (DNS-SD)\n   profile for discovering LLM API services on a local link.  An\n   advertiser publishes the service location and a small set of API\n   connection parameters.  A discovery client resolves these records and\n   offers the resulting connection information to an AI application or\n   its configuration adapter.  Subsequent LLM API requests are sent\n   directly to the discovered service, not through the advertiser or\n   discovery client.  Discovery does not establish trust, distribute\n   credentials, select models, or define an application's configuration\n   file format.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-05T10:38:23Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 162124, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-cui-dawn-local-llm-api-discovery", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 13, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-cui-dawn-local-llm-api-discovery/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/171610/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-11-01T10:38:23Z", "title": "Discovery of LLM API Services in Local Networks", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   The charter of the Discovery of Agents With Names (DAWN) working\n   group asks it to specify the minimum public discovery information\n   that a client obtains when it discovers an AI resource.  An\n   individual draft on DAWN terminology defines Minimum Discoverable\n   Information (MDI) as the minimum amount of information an entity\n   needs to provide to become discoverable, distinguishes it from the\n   Minimum Viable Dataset (MVD) that a discovery mechanism needs as\n   input, and gives only examples of the content of either.  Individual\n   drafts on the DAWN problem statement and requirements call for a\n   standardized minimum structure and list what it must at least\n   contain; in its absence, emerging discovery formats risk each\n   defining its own minimal metadata, with descriptors that do not\n   interoperate across organizational or protocol boundaries.\n\n   This document addresses the minimum public discovery information as a\n   client obtains it and as a publisher provides it, and its relation to\n   the input of discovery; registration mechanisms are outside its\n   scope.  Using the distinction RFC 3444 draws between information\n   models and data models, it describes the information involved in\n   publication, lookup, and response; proposes a procedure that derives\n   what is minimal for each deployment context from the scope of the\n   charter; applies the procedure to the charter's three deployment\n   contexts: a local network, an organization, and the domain of a\n   cooperating party; and proposes a set of elements with the minimal\n   constraints that hold among them.  Information can be supplied by the\n   discovery response, by context the client already has, or by binding\n   rules.  This document is input to the DAWN architecture and protocol\n   work; normative definitions that affect interoperability belong to\n   the corresponding Standards Track documents.  It is a mapping target\n   for richer descriptors.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-06T12:01:53Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 157696, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-cui-dawn-mdi-model", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 31, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-cui-dawn-mdi-model/", "rev": "02", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/165502/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171578/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171859/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-11-02T12:01:53Z", "title": "Minimum Public Discovery Information: Requirements and Common Semantics", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document specifies DNS for AI Discovery (DNS-AID), a method for\n   publishing AI agents in the Domain Name System (DNS) so that other\n   agents can discover them.  It defines three discovery use cases,\n   ordered by increasing latency and computational cost.  In the known-\n   agent case, the requestor knows both the agent and its domain, and a\n   single DNS query returns connectivity information and key metadata.\n   In the known-organization case, the requestor knows the domain but\n   not which agent provides a required capability, and queries a well-\n   known organizational index to get connectivity information to enable\n   a capability search of a catalog, agent registry or database.  In the\n   known-capability case, the requestor knows only the required\n   capability and must rely on an external directory or search service.\n   This document addresses the known-agent and known-organization cases.\n   The known-capability case is out of scope, but search and cataloging\n   services can be built from the organizational index defined here.\n   Once a client has learned an organization’s agents, later\n   interactions can use the known-agent case, relying on a minimal,\n   cacheable record set that each organization controls.  DNS-AID uses\n   Service Binding (SVCB) records, optionally with DNS-Based Service\n   Discovery (DNS-SD) labels, DNSSEC, and DANE TLSA records.  This gives\n   agents direct connections that no third party mediates and works the\n   same way on public and private networks.  No new resource record\n   types, opcodes, or response codes are introduced.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-06T14:58:24Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 162219, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-mozleywilliams-dawn-dnsaid", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 24, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-mozleywilliams-dawn-dnsaid/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/171927/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-11-02T14:58:24Z", "title": "DNS for AI Discovery", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   The DAWN working group is defining how a client gets the Minimum\n   Public Discovery Information (MDI) about an AI resource before it\n   interacts with it.  The charter covers three settings: a local\n   network, an organization, and cooperating parties over the public\n   Internet.\n\n   One proposal for the minimum, the MDI draft, finds that it is the\n   same in all three settings.  This document points out that three\n   other things still change with locality: how much a client can learn\n   at or right after discovery, what the client can reasonably assume\n   from where it is looking, and how well it can check who published the\n   information.  The last one does not simply get better or worse from\n   local to remote.  Records under a party's domain can be validated\n   with DNSSEC, but local multicast advertisements cannot be\n   authenticated, and proxies and catalogs can be authenticated only as\n   themselves.\n\n   This is a discussion contribution.  It offers some points for the\n   architecture and MDI documents: how local detail relates to the MDI\n   minimum, whether a client should keep track of where a result came\n   from, what to do when local and remote results disagree, and how much\n   weight to give physical proximity as a sign of trust.  It proposes no\n   protocol.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-06T17:55:13Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 162254, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-abboud-dawn-locality-gradient", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 10, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-abboud-dawn-locality-gradient/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/172034/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-11-02T17:55:13Z", "title": "Locality in Agent Discovery", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document presents a use case and problem statement for the\n   initial discovery of AI agents supporting end-to-end communication\n   services within an organization.  The use case considers AI agents\n   associated with different functions of the Internet protocol suite,\n   including application, transport, Internet-layer, and related\n   network-operation functions.  These agents may share general\n   capabilities but differ in their communication-related operational\n   scopes and responsibilities.  The document examines the minimum\n   public discovery information needed to distinguish such agents within\n   a known organizational discovery scope.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-06T19:37:47Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 162271, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-jadoon-dawn-layered-agent-discovery", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 8, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-jadoon-dawn-layered-agent-discovery/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/172091/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-11-02T19:37:47Z", "title": "Discovery of AI Agents for Communication Services: Use Case and Problem Statement", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}]}